## SECTION 2 — NEXT.JS APP ROUTER REVIEW

### 2.1 Massive `"use client"` Pages Destroy SSR Benefits
**Severity: Critical**

Almost every feature page is a `"use client"` monolith:

- [BookUpdatesPage.tsx](file:///c:/Users/MyPc/Documents/GitHub/front-nextJs/src/features/bookUpdates/BookUpdatesPage.tsx) — 948 lines, `"use client"`, entire page is client JS
- [CollectionDetail.tsx](file:///c:/Users/MyPc/Documents/GitHub/front-nextJs/src/components/collection/CollectionDetail.tsx) — 548 lines, `"use client"`
- [HomePage.tsx](file:///c:/Users/MyPc/Documents/GitHub/front-nextJs/src/features/Home/HomePage.tsx) — likely also `"use client"`

The route pages in `src/app/(public)/book-updates/page.tsx` are just:
```tsx
export default function Page() {
  return <BookUpdatesPage />; // ← entire page is client-rendered
}
```

**Impact**:
- **Zero SSR**: The initial HTML is an empty shell. Search engines see nothing. TTFB advantage of RSC is wasted.
- **Huge JS bundle**: 948 lines of JSX + Swiper + Ant Design Select + Lucide icons — ALL shipped as client JS.
- **Contradicts the entire purpose of App Router**: You're paying the complexity tax of RSC without any benefit.

**Correct approach**: 
```tsx
// app/(public)/book-updates/page.tsx — Server Component
export default async function Page() {
  // Prefetch calendar data on server
  const queryClient = new QueryClient();
  await queryClient.prefetchQuery({
    queryKey: queryKeys.bookUpdates.calendar('all', 'all'),
    queryFn: () => fetchBookUpdateCalendar({ scope: 'all', contentType: 'all' }),
  });

  return (
    <HydrationBoundary state={dehydrate(queryClient)}>
      <BookUpdatesHero />           {/* Server Component: static hero */}
      <BookUpdatesFilters />         {/* Client: interactive filters */}
      <BookUpdatesCalendarStrip />   {/* Client: swiper calendar */}
      <BookUpdatesList />            {/* Client: dynamic book list */}
    </HydrationBoundary>
  );
}
```

